1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,275
Welcome to Module Fifteen.

2
00:00:02,275 --> 00:00:06,412
Social insects invented division of labour long before we did.

3
00:00:06,412 --> 00:00:08,937
Nurses, foragers, guards.

4
00:00:08,937 --> 00:00:16,337
Each does one job, with the tools its body gives it, and the colony gets far more done than the same number of generalists would.

5
00:00:16,337 --> 00:00:20,425
There's a second idea from ecology I want you to hold on to today.

6
00:00:20,425 --> 00:00:23,300
Predators keep prey populations honest.

7
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:25,712
The careless and the weak get culled.

8
00:00:25,712 --> 00:00:28,562
A system with no predator slowly drifts.

9
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:30,562
Agents use both ideas.

10
00:00:30,562 --> 00:00:32,512
The first is the subagent.

11
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:36,425
Think of it as a specialist worker with its own context window.

12
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:41,237
It gets a short brief, does one job, and hands back only its result.

13
00:00:41,237 --> 00:00:43,450
That keeps your main conversation clean.

14
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:46,462
And crucially, you can restrict its tools.

15
00:00:46,462 --> 00:00:55,450
A reviewer that can read files and run tests, but cannot edit anything, is a reviewer you can trust not to quietly fix things and call it a pass.

16
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:58,900
The second idea is the predator, the adversary.

17
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:03,487
Two of today's sources describe the same three moments when it should wake up.

18
00:01:03,487 --> 00:01:05,487
Before a plan is locked in.

19
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:12,250
When the same error comes back twice, because that is usually a sign you're hiding a symptom instead of fixing a cause.

20
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:14,912
And before anyone says the word done.

21
00:01:14,912 --> 00:01:21,287
Claude Code can also run whole teams, where a lead coordinates several sessions that message each other.

22
00:01:21,287 --> 00:01:24,237
The documentation is honest about the price.

23
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:34,362
Teams use far more tokens, they suit research, review and debugging with competing hypotheses, and three focused teammates often beat five scattered ones.

24
00:01:34,362 --> 00:01:35,787
Now the trap.

25
00:01:35,787 --> 00:01:40,362
If you put four reviewers in one shared conversation, they converge.

26
00:01:40,362 --> 00:01:45,912
The first one writes a finding, the others read it, and all four reports say the same thing.

27
00:01:45,912 --> 00:01:50,925
One of our sources calls this paying four times for one opinion with three echoes.

28
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:55,650
The fix is to give each reviewer its own context and a different lens.

29
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:59,137
And this is exactly where the caste metaphor breaks.

30
00:01:59,137 --> 00:02:05,225
Real castes differ in genes, age and experience, so their mistakes are partly independent.

31
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:10,600
Cloned agents share the same model and the same training, so they share the same blind spots.

32
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:13,112
If you want diversity, you have to design it in.

33
00:02:13,112 --> 00:02:22,512
In the lab, you'll build a read-only reviewer for your field data cleaning code, with three lenses: data meaning, code correctness and provenance.

34
00:02:22,512 --> 00:02:25,637
Then you'll plant a bug that any ecologist would hate.

35
00:02:25,637 --> 00:02:30,237
You'll ask the main agent to quietly drop every row where the count is zero.

36
00:02:30,237 --> 00:02:32,587
A zero is not missing data.

37
00:02:32,587 --> 00:02:34,975
It's an absence, and it matters.

38
00:02:34,975 --> 00:02:37,150
Let's see whether your predator catches it.

39
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:40,137
Open the reading, then go build your reviewer.

